# Calibration store seed - every value the synthetic feed is calibrated with, and the only place
# those values are defined. Applied by `seed.sh` (beside this file), which the `calibration-seeder`
# Compose service runs: it strips comment and blank lines (redis-cli does not understand comments),
# dry-runs the rest with redis-cli against scratch DB 15, then applies it to the live DB 0 only if
# that run had no error. It fails on any `(error)` reply, so the adapter (which waits for the
# seeder to succeed) never starts on a failed seed.
#
# The whole file is one MULTI/EXEC transaction. Its first queued command deletes every existing
# calibration key, so a reader sees either the previous seeding or this one - never a mix - and an
# instrument removed from this file disappears from the store. MULTI/EXEC alone does not make a
# broken script harmless: a command rejected while queueing aborts the whole transaction
# (EXECABORT), but one that fails while EXEC runs (e.g. an HSET with an odd field/value count) does
# not roll back the rest, delete included. So the seeder (`seed.sh`, beside this file) dry-runs the
# script against scratch DB 15 first and applies it to the live DB 0 only if that run had no error.
#
# Key layout (owned by the `calibration-store` capability; read by the adapter's CalibrationStore):
#   symbology                              hash: venue symbol -> file symbol
#   instrument:<venue>                     hash: pip_size, quote_currency, initial_price
#   calib:<venue>:<quantity>:family        fitted family name
#   calib:<venue>:<quantity>:param_count   number of parameters
#   calib:<venue>:<quantity>:param:<n>     hash: name, value, unit (n from 0, scipy fit order)
#   calib:symbols / calib:quantities       discovery sets
#
# Values are in STORED units: returns in quote units, spreads in pips (multiples of pip_size),
# tick intervals in milliseconds. Only `loc` and `scale` carry a unit; every other parameter is a
# dimensionless shape parameter. The adapter converts by each parameter's `unit` field alone.
# Numbers are plain decimal strings (never scientific notation).

MULTI

# Delete the previous seeding (calib:*, instrument:*, symbology) inside the same transaction.
EVAL "for _,p in ipairs(ARGV) do for _,k in ipairs(redis.call('KEYS',p)) do redis.call('DEL',k) end end return 0" 0 calib:* instrument:* symbology

# Venue symbol -> file symbol. FX pairs use `BASE/QUOTE`; the CFDs use the venue's
# `<ticker>.<market>/<quote>` notation.
HSET symbology AAPL.US/USD AAPLUSUSD ARKQ.US/USD ARKQUSUSD AUD/JPY AUDJPY AUD/USD AUDUSD EUR/GBP EURGBP EUR/JPY EURJPY EUR/USD EURUSD GBP/JPY GBPJPY GBP/USD GBPUSD NZD/JPY NZDJPY NZD/USD NZDUSD USA500.IDX/USD USA500IDXUSD USATECH.IDX/USD USATECHIDXUSD USD/CAD USDCAD USD/CHF USDCHF USD/CNH USDCNH USD/JPY USDJPY

# Per-instrument metadata.
#
# `initial_price` is the first `Bid` of each symbol's tick sample (`data/*.parquet`, week of
# 2026-08-31) - the Bid, not the Bid/Ask mid, since averaging the two adds sub-grain noise.
#
# `pip_size` is the instrument's minimum price increment: the fewest decimal places every `Bid` in
# that sample round-trips through unchanged. Rounding to fewer decimals than a price's true
# precision changes it; rounding to at least that many is a no-op, so the smallest such count is
# the sample's quoting grain - robust to a price with trailing zeros (`1.10000`), which survives
# rounding at any count and so never lowers the answer. Two groups fall out: every FX pair quoted
# against JPY, and all four CFDs, quote to the 3rd decimal; every other FX pair to the 5th.
#
# This is finer than the classical FX "pip" (the 4th decimal on non-JPY pairs, the 2nd on JPY
# pairs): the feed quotes one digit past it - a pipette on non-JPY pairs. `pip_size` means the
# grain actually quoted, whatever it is called, and spread parameters are stored in multiples of
# it. The CFDs have no FX pip convention at all; the 3rd decimal is the broker's CFD convention,
# independent of price level (a $319 stock and a 29,290-point index both quote to 3 decimals).
#
# `quote_currency` is the part of the venue symbol after `/`.
HSET instrument:AAPL.US/USD pip_size 0.001 quote_currency USD initial_price 319.426
HSET instrument:ARKQ.US/USD pip_size 0.001 quote_currency USD initial_price 121.566
HSET instrument:AUD/JPY pip_size 0.001 quote_currency JPY initial_price 114.496
HSET instrument:AUD/USD pip_size 0.00001 quote_currency USD initial_price 0.71659
HSET instrument:EUR/GBP pip_size 0.00001 quote_currency GBP initial_price 0.85584
HSET instrument:EUR/JPY pip_size 0.001 quote_currency JPY initial_price 185.215
HSET instrument:EUR/USD pip_size 0.00001 quote_currency USD initial_price 1.15922
HSET instrument:GBP/JPY pip_size 0.001 quote_currency JPY initial_price 216.402
HSET instrument:GBP/USD pip_size 0.00001 quote_currency USD initial_price 1.35444
HSET instrument:NZD/JPY pip_size 0.001 quote_currency JPY initial_price 94.556
HSET instrument:NZD/USD pip_size 0.00001 quote_currency USD initial_price 0.59177
HSET instrument:USA500.IDX/USD pip_size 0.001 quote_currency USD initial_price 7680.869
HSET instrument:USATECH.IDX/USD pip_size 0.001 quote_currency USD initial_price 29290.786
HSET instrument:USD/CAD pip_size 0.00001 quote_currency CAD initial_price 1.38902
HSET instrument:USD/CHF pip_size 0.00001 quote_currency CHF initial_price 0.80852
HSET instrument:USD/CNH pip_size 0.00001 quote_currency CNH initial_price 6.72294
HSET instrument:USD/JPY pip_size 0.001 quote_currency JPY initial_price 159.773

# How the three fit tables below were produced (an offline run with `scipy`/`numpy` in a
# throwaway virtualenv - neither is a project dependency): for each symbol, five candidate
# families were fitted by MLE on a 200k-row subsample (the largest file, USATECHIDXUSD, runs to
# ~1.7M ticks), ranked by AIC computed over the full sample (log-likelihood penalized for
# parameter count; lower is better), and cross-checked with a KS test. The winner's family and
# parameters are the row. Real tick series are long quiet stretches punctuated by jumps (session
# gaps, news) - heavy-tailed, not i.i.d. Gaussian - which is why no quantity picks Normal or
# Exponential anywhere (cf. Cont, R. (2001), "Empirical properties of asset returns: stylized
# facts and statistical issues").
#
# Returns: consecutive `Bid` differences, stored in quote units (price units, no conversion). Candidates: Normal, Student-t, Laplace,
# Logistic, Cauchy. Every symbol rejects Normal decisively; 13 pick Laplace, 4 pick Student-t.
#
# - Laplace(loc, scale): every fitted `loc` is exactly 0 - the median return is a flat tick, and
#   Laplace's MLE location is the sample median. `scale` is the mean absolute deviation.
# - Student-t(df, loc, scale): lower `df` means fatter tails. USATECHIDXUSD and USDJPY sit near
#   df 3 (variance barely finite); USA500IDXUSD's df ~52 is nearly Normal - the only symbol where
#   Normal even placed second.
# - ARKQUSUSD's extreme kurtosis (~11,600) is real, not a data bug: its three largest returns all
#   land on the 16:59:59 -> next-day 10:30:00 overnight-close boundary.
#
# Runner-up and AIC margin (2nd - best) per symbol, for anyone revisiting a close call:
# AAPLUSUSD Student-t 8,004; ARKQUSUSD Cauchy 11,933; AUDJPY Logistic 36,705; AUDUSD Logistic
# 6,144; EURGBP Student-t 4,482; EURJPY Student-t 68,855; EURUSD Logistic 3,395; GBPJPY Student-t
# 12,335; GBPUSD Logistic 17,434; NZDJPY Student-t 41,117; NZDUSD Logistic 16,017; USA500IDXUSD
# Normal 18,505; USATECHIDXUSD Laplace 52,609; USDCAD Student-t 10,814; USDCHF Student-t 13,254;
# USDCNH Student-t 18,935; USDJPY Laplace 3,538.

SET calib:AAPL.US/USD:return:family laplace
SET calib:AAPL.US/USD:return:param_count 2
HSET calib:AAPL.US/USD:return:param:0 name loc value 0 unit quote
HSET calib:AAPL.US/USD:return:param:1 name scale value 0.0171211 unit quote

